.TH NROFF 1
.SH NAME
nroff \- text formatting
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nroff
[ option ] ...  [ file ] ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nroff
formats text in the named
.I files
for printing on typewriter-like devices.
Its capabilities are described in the ``Nroff User's Manual,'' in the
.IR "Document Processing Guide" .
.PP
If no
.I file
argument is present, the standard input is used.
An argument
consisting of a single minus (\-) is taken to be a file name corresponding
to the standard input, and is often used to attach special macro files to a
text file for processing.
The options, which may appear in any order so
long as they appear before the files, are:
.TP 8
.BI \-o list
Print only pages whose page numbers appear in the comma-separated
.I list
of numbers and ranges.
For example,
.B \-o2,6
prints only the second and sixth pages.
A range
.IR N \- M
means
pages
.I N
through
.IR M ;
an initial
.RI \- N
means from the beginning
to page
.IR N ;
and a final
.IR N \-
means from
.I N
to the end.
.TP
.BI \-n N
Number the first generated page
.IR N .
.TP
.BI \-s N
Stop every
.I N
pages.
.B nroff
will halt prior to every
.I N
pages
(default
.IR N =1)
to allow paper loading or changing, and will
resume upon receipt of a newline.
.TP
.BI \-m name
Prepend the macro file
.BI /usr/lib/tmac/tmac. name
to the input
.IR files .
This is a convenient way to specify different page formatting
macro's from the command line.
.TP
.BI \-r aN
Set register
.I a
(one-character) to
.IR N .
.TP
.B \-i
Read standard input after the input files are exhausted.
.TP
.B \-q
Invoke the simultaneous input-output mode of the
.B rd
request,
as described in the ``Nroff User's Manual.''
.TP
.BI \-T name
Prepare output for specified terminal.
Known
.I names
are
.B 37
for the (default) Teletype Corporation Model 37 terminal,
.B tn300
for the GE TermiNet 300 (or any terminal without
half-line capability), and
.B 450
for the DASI-450 (Diablo
Hyterm).
.TP
.B \-e
Produce equally-spaced words in adjusted lines, using full terminal
resolution.
.TP
.B \-h
Use output tabs during horizontal spacing to speed output and
reduce output character count.
Tab settings are assumed to
be every 8 nominal character widths.
.SH EXAMPLES
Run off document ``doc.1'' with \-ms macros, formatted for Diablo
Hyterm and send it to the terminal screen:
.IP
nroff \-T450 \-ms doc.1
.PP
Run off document
.IR proposal ,
with macro set
.I myron
and send it to the
printer spooler with a no-header flag:
.IP
nroff \-mmyron \-T450 proposal | lpr \-c
.SH FILES
.nf
/usr/lib/suftab      suffix hyphenation tables
/tmp/ta*             temporary file
/usr/lib/tmac/tmac.* standard macro files
/usr/lib/term/*      terminal driving tables
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.I "Document Processing Guide"
.br
neqn(1), tbl(1)
.br
col(1)
.SH "MS MACRO SET"
This package of
.B nroff
macro definitions provides a canned formatting
facility for technical papers in various formats.
When producing 2-column
output on a terminal, filter the output through
.BR col (1).
.PP
To use this set, the
.B \-ms
flag must be given on the
.B nroff
command line.
See ``Using the \-ms Macros'' in the
.I "Document Processing Guide"
for
details.
.PP
The macro requests are defined below.
Many
.B nroff
requests are unsafe
in conjunction with this package, however these requests may be used
with impunity after the first .PP:
.PP
.RS
.nf
.ta .8i
\&.bp	begin new page
\&.br	break output line here
\&.sp n	insert n spacing lines
\&.ls n	(line spacing) n=1 single, n=2 double space
\&.na	no alignment of right margin
.fi
.RE
.PP
Output of the
.B neqn
and
.BR tbl (1)
preprocessors for equations and tables is
acceptable as input.
.PP
The following requests are available:
.PP
.nf
.ta 1i 1.7i 2.4i
REQUEST	INITIAL	CAUSE	EXPLANATION
	VALUE	BREAK
\&.1C	yes	yes	One column format on a new page.
\&.2C	no	yes	Two column format.
\&.AB	no	yes	Begin abstract.
\&.AE	-	yes	End abstract.
\&.AI	no	yes	Author's institution follows.
			Suppressed in TM.
\&.AT	no	yes	Print `Attached' and turn off line
			filling.
\&.AU \fIx y\fR	no	yes	Author's name follows. \fIx\fR is
			location and \fIy\fR is extension,
			ignored except in TM.
\&.B \fIx\fR	no	no	Print \fIx\fR in boldface; if no argument
			switch to boldface.
\&.B1	no	yes	Begin text to be enclosed in a box.
\&.B2	no	yes	End text to be boxed & print it.
\&.BT	date	no	Bottom title, automatically invoked
			at foot of page. May be redefined.
\&.BX \fIx\fR	no	no	Print \fIx\fR in a box.
\&.CS \fIx...\fR	-	yes	Cover sheet info if TM format,
			suppressed otherwise. Arguments are
			number of text pages, other pages,
			total pages, figures, tables,
			references.
\&.CT	no	yes	Print `Copies to' and enter no-fill
			mode.
\&.DA \fIx\fR	nroff	no	`Date line' at bottom of page is \fIx\fR.
			Default is today.
\&.DE	-	yes	End displayed text. Implies .KE.
\&.DS \fIx\fR	no	yes	Start of displayed text, to appear
			verbatim line-by-line. \fIx\fR =I for
			indented display (default), =L for
			left-justified on the page, =C for
			centered, =B for make
			left-justified block, then center
			whole block. Implies .KS.
\&.EG	no	-	Print document in BTL format for
			`Engineer's Notes.' Must be first.
\&.EN	-	yes	Space after equation produced by
			\fBneqn\fR.
\&.EQ \fIx y\fR	-	yes	Precede equation; break out and add
			space. Equation number is \fIy\fR. The
			optional argument \fIx\fR may be \fII\fR to
			indent equation (default), \fIL\fR to
			left-adjust the equation, or \fIC\fR to
			center the equation.
\&.FE	-	yes	End footnote.
\&.FS	no	no	Start footnote. The note will be
			moved to the bottom of the page.
\&.I \fIx\fR	no	no	Italicize (underline) \fIx\fR; if \fIx\fR
			missing, italic text follows.
\&.IM	no	no	Print document in BTL format for an
			internal memorandum. Must be first.
\&.IP \fIx y\fR	no	yes	Start indented paragraph, with
			hanging tag \fIx\fR. Indentation is \fIy\fR ens
			(default 5).
\&.KE	-	yes	End keep. Put kept text on next
			page if not enough room.
\&.KF	no	yes	Start floating keep. If the kept
			text must be moved to the next
			page, float later text back to this
			page.
\&.KS	no	yes	Start keeping following text.
\&.LG	no	no	Make letters larger.
\&.LP	yes	yes	Start left-blocked paragraph.
\&.MF	-	-	Print document in BTL format for
			`Memorandum for File.' Must be
			first.
\&.MR	-	-	Print document in BTL format for
			`Memorandum for Record.' Must be
			first.
\&.ND	date	no	Use date supplied (if any) only in
			special BTL format positions; omit
			from page footer.
\&.NH \fBn\fR	-	yes	Same as .SH, with section number
			supplied automatically. Numbers are
			multilevel, like 1.2.3, where \fIn\fR
			tells what level is wanted (default
			is 1).
\&.NL	yes	no	Make letters normal size.
\&.OK	-	yes	`Other keywords' for TM cover sheet
			follow.
\&.PP	no	yes	Begin paragraph. First line
			indented.
\&.PT	pg #	-	Page title, automatically invoked
			at top of page. May be redefined.
\&.QE	-	yes	End quoted (indented and shorter)
			material.
\&.QP	-	yes	Begin single paragraph which is
			indented and shorter.
\&.QS	-	yes	Begin quoted (indented and shorter)
			material.
\&.R	yes	no	Roman text follows.
\&.RE	-	yes	End relative indent level.
\&.RP	no	-	Cover sheet and first page for
			released paper. Must precede other
			requests.
\&.RS	-	yes	Start level of relative
			indentation. Following .IP's are
			measured from current indentation.
\&.SG \fIx\fR	no	yes	Insert signature(s) of author(s),
			ignored except in TM. \fIx\fR is the
			reference line (initials of author
			and typist).
\&.SH	-	yes	Section head follows, font
			automatically bold.
\&.SM	no	no	Make letters smaller.
\&.TA \fIx\fR...	5...	no	Set tabs in ens. Default is 5 10 15
			...
\&.TE	-	yes	End table.
\&.TH	-	yes	End heading section of table.
\&.TL	no	yes	Title follows.
\&.TM \fIx\fR...	no	-	Print document in BTL technical
			memorandum format. Arguments are TM
			number, (quoted list of) case
			number(s), and file number. Must
			precede other requests.
\&.TR \fIx\fR	-	-	Print in BTL technical report
			format; report number is \fIx\fR. Must be
			first.
\&.TS \fIx\fR	-	yes	Begin table; if \fIx\fR is \fIH\fR table has
			repeated heading.
\&.UL \fIx\fR	-	no	Underline argument.
\&.UX	-	no	`UNIX'; first time used, adds
			footnote `UNIX is a trademark of
			Bell Laboratories.'
.fi
.sp
